"Relates a customer segment's jobs, pains and gains to the offer's products, pain relievers and gain creators.\n\nUse when: Investigate whether an offer addresses meaningful customer needs. Expose unsupported claims about product-customer fit.\n\nAvoid when: The question is the complete business model, including partners and revenue.\n\nVisual grammar: A customer profile separates jobs, pains and gains. A value map separates products, pain relievers and gain creators. Selective correspondence marks explain proposed fit without joining every note.\n\nBuild: Choose one segment and one relevant situation. Record customer evidence before designing the value map. Link the most significant needs to specific offer mechanisms and tests.\n\nCheck: Customer jobs remain independent of the proposed solution. Each claimed benefit addresses a stated pain or gain. Observed evidence and untested fit hypotheses remain distinguishable.\n\nPitfalls: Inventing customer needs to justify existing features. Claiming the offer must solve every possible customer problem.\n\nExample: A commuter's job of reaching work reliably is matched to delay alerts and backup routes, with convenience claims marked for testing.\n\nAlternatives: Business Model Canvas, Job map."
